\paragraph{No evidence of distribution matching.}
An early single-seed OOD anomaly was traced to a checkpoint resume artifact (Appendix~\ref{app:resume_artifact}); three-seed from-scratch evaluation confirms OOD gains exceed in-distribution ($|\Delta| \leq 0.029$; \S\ref{sec:robustness}).

\paragraph{Practical guidance.}
With a calibrated discriminative detector (AUC ${\geq}$ 0.94, low ECE), adaptive $\bstar$ with $\tau{=}0.7$ and $\nmin{=}\lfloor B/2 \rfloor$ provides a zero-tuning baseline that improves over uniform at $\rho \geq 0.4$.
If compute allows multi-epoch training on filtered data, D\&R retains a small edge at equal content epochs ($\Delta{=}0.017$, $N{=}3$; Figure~\ref{fig:dr_dual_curve}).
Without a calibrated detector, uniform training is preferable to reweighting with uncalibrated scores (\S\ref{sec:robustness}).
